For healthtech backend task monitoring, choose tools that can answer the failure in front of you: an import watchdog catches “the task ran, but no usable result arrived,” while health check APIs and synthetic checks answer “can this endpoint respond right now?” They are complementary, but they do not produce the same evidence or belong on the same alert.



Approach
Evidence it produces
Pick it when
Main boundary




Import watchdog
A scheduled run produced a count, timestamp, and outcome
Missing or stale business data is the incident
It cannot prove that a user-facing API is reachable


Synthetic health check
A request crossed a network boundary and received an expected response
Availability of a route or dependency is the incident
A healthy response can hide a stalled importer


Pull-based metrics and rules
Time series showing freshness and error state
The team needs history, aggregation, and alert routing
It needs an exporter or collector to observe the job



The least complex design is a small Node.js watchdog that records one outcome per import, plus a health endpoint that reports process and dependency readiness. The watchdog owns freshness. The endpoint owns reachability. Cost attribution becomes much clearer when each alert has one observable claim.
Start with the sentence an on-call engineer should be able to complete: “We are paging because ___.” For a scheduled import, useful completions include “the 06:00 run has not produced a result,” “the source returned zero records three times,” or “the importer is failing validation.” “The service is up” is a different sentence.
The trap is treating a successful HTTP response as proof that the backend task succeeded.
Keep the claims separate.
That distinction matters in healthcare workflows. A process can return HTTP 200 while it has an empty payload, an old cursor, or a parser that discarded every row. A process can also be healthy while its public route is unreachable from outside the cluster. Treating those cases as one binary status creates noisy incidents and hides the cost of the check that found them.
I use two timestamps in the import record: startedAt and completedAt. I also keep recordsAccepted, sourceBatchId, and a normalized outcome. The timestamp used for freshness is the completion time, not the scheduler's planned time. That small choice avoids paging on a slow run that still delivered valid data.
Here is the boundary in words: the scheduler emits an event; the importer turns the event into an outcome; the watchdog measures the age of the latest successful outcome; the health endpoint reports whether the process can serve requests. An alerting rule consumes one of those signals and names its owner.
The health route should be cheap and bounded. It can check that the process is accepting requests and that required configuration is present. A readiness route may test a database connection with a short timeout. It should not start an import, scan a whole result table, or wait for a third-party source. Those operations turn a probe into an accidental workload. The API proves reachability; the task record proves freshness.
The watchdog can carry the slower, domain-specific check. This TypeScript sketch keeps the external interface generic and makes the decision visible in the data rather than hiding it in a vendor-specific callback:
type ImportOutcome = {
  startedAt: string;
  completedAt: string;
  recordsAccepted: number;
  sourceBatchId: string;
  outcome: "success" | "empty" | "failed";
};

type ImportStore = {
  latestSuccessful(): Promise<ImportOutcome | null>;
  save(outcome: ImportOutcome): Promise<void>;
};

export async function runImport(store: ImportStore): Promise<ImportOutcome> {
  const startedAt = new Date().toISOString();

  try {
    const batch = await fetchSourceBatch();
    const accepted = validateAndCount(batch.records);
    const outcome: ImportOutcome = {
      startedAt,
      completedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      recordsAccepted: accepted,
      sourceBatchId: batch.id,
      outcome: accepted === 0 ? "empty" : "success",
    };

    await store.save(outcome);
    return outcome;
  } catch (error) {
    const outcome: ImportOutcome = {
      startedAt,
      completedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      recordsAccepted: 0,
      sourceBatchId: "unknown",
      outcome: "failed",
    };

    await store.save(outcome);
    throw error;
  }
}

declare function fetchSourceBatch(): Promise<{
  id: string;
  records: unknown[];
}>;

declare function validateAndCount(records: unknown[]): number;

The empty outcome is deliberate. It is not automatically an incident: some source systems legitimately publish no records. The policy should decide whether one empty batch is informational and whether repeated empties, measured over a defined window, are actionable. That policy belongs beside the data contract, where a reviewer can change it without rewriting probe code.
For the freshness calculation, keep the threshold in the same time unit as the schedule. If imports are expected every 30 minutes, a rule might mark the stream stale after 75 minutes. The extra 45 minutes is a policy choice for source delay, retries, and deployment pauses; it is not a universal magic number. Record the reason for the margin.
Cost attribution is easier when checks are assigned to work they can explain. A watchdog run has an execution cost and a data-processing cost. A synthetic check has request, network, and probe-frequency costs. Pull-based metrics add collection and retention costs. None of these should be disguised as a single “monitoring” line item.
That is a real trade-off. A backend tool that records every task outcome gives better failure evidence, but it adds persistence and retention work. A health-check API is lighter, but it cannot expose stale business data. Pick the cheaper evidence only when it still proves the incident you care about.
The product landscape reflects these shapes, without making the products interchangeable. Healthchecks.io is built around reporting scheduled-job completion through a ping. Prometheus is built around time-series collection and rule evaluation. Grafana Cloud includes metrics and synthetic monitoring as separate observability capabilities. Better Stack documents uptime checks alongside incident workflows. These are useful examples of different boundaries; the right comparison is the signal and its retention, not a feature-count contest.
A practical allocation table might assign the importer to the data platform budget, external route probes to platform reliability, and long-lived metric retention to shared observability. The labels matter more than the dashboard. They let a team ask why a check runs, who acts on it, and how long its evidence must remain available.
The useful review is a short cost-and-evidence exercise, not a hunt for the longest feature list. Draw one line for the scheduled task and one for the public API. On the task line, record the run frequency, the outcome record, the freshness window, and the retention period needed for investigation. On the API line, record the probe interval, the network locations that matter, the response timeout, and the amount of history needed to see an availability pattern. If a proposed tool adds another signal, write down the decision that signal changes. If nobody can name that decision, the signal is overhead. If the signal replaces a manual check during a data incident, it may be worth the operational cost even when it does not reduce compute usage. This is the part that prevents a cheap-looking health endpoint from becoming an expensive source of false confidence.
OpenTelemetry helps keep the vocabulary portable: logs describe discrete events, while metrics describe measurements over time. Emit the import outcome as a structured event and expose freshness as a measurement. Do not force a high-cardinality batch identifier into a metric label. Put it in the event or trace context instead. That preserves queryability without turning every source batch into a new time series.
Write the failure cases before choosing a monitoring service. For each case, specify the observed signal, the owner, the delay allowed, and the action. Then test the importer with a valid batch, an empty batch, malformed records, a timeout, and a retry that eventually succeeds. Health routes need their own tests for a ready process, a missing dependency, and a timeout.
The probe response should be stable enough for automation, but useful enough for an engineer. This is a deliberately small example:
type Readiness = {
  status: "ready" | "not_ready";
  checks: Record<string, "pass" | "fail">;
};

export async function readiness(
  checkDatabase: () => Promise<boolean>,
): Promise<Readiness> {
  const database = await Promise.race([
    checkDatabase(),
    new Promise<boolean>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(false), 800)),
  ]);

  return {
    status: database ? "ready" : "not_ready",
    checks: { database: database ? "pass" : "fail" },
  };
}

Do not let a failed readiness check erase the last successful import timestamp. Those are separate facts. Alert routing can then distinguish “the API cannot accept traffic” from “fresh data is missing,” and a post-incident review can identify which check created the page.
That separation pays off during review.
For a real deployment, also define deduplication and recovery behavior. One alert should recover when the same condition clears, not every time a probe happens to pass. Keep a small sample of the raw event around long enough to answer what the importer received and accepted. That evidence is often more valuable than another dashboard panel.
An import watchdog cannot tell you that a clinician can reach the API. A synthetic health check cannot tell you that the last successful import contained complete, current data. Metrics can show a trend, but they cannot replace the payload-level evidence needed to investigate a rejected batch. A lightweight health check is a poor fit for a data-freshness incident; a full watchdog is unnecessary for a route-only availability question.
There is also a human limit. Every distinct failure claim needs an owner and a runbook. If the team cannot say what a notification means or what action follows, increasing probe frequency will only increase interruptions. Start with the smallest pair of signals that separates freshness from reachability, then add detail when an observed failure proves the need.
https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/logs/
https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/overview/
https://prometheus.io/docs/concepts/data_model/
https://healthchecks.io/docs/
https://grafana.com/docs/grafana-cloud/testing/synthetic-monitoring/
https://betterstack.com/docs/uptime/

I have watched the same failure play out in real-time features more than once. One server, one room, everything works. You add a second instance behind a load balancer to handle more connections, and half your users stop receiving messages. Nobody changed the room code. Nobody changed the gateway. The system just got bigger, and the thing that worked at one instance quietly stopped working at two.
The cause is simple, and that is what makes it dangerous. Socket.io rooms live in memory by default. Instance A knows its own sockets. Instance B knows its own sockets. Neither knows the other's. When instance A broadcasts to a room, it only talks to the sockets it can see.
This post is the fix I reach for in NestJS: the Redis adapter, the sticky-session rule that makes it actually work, and a room pattern that stops you from rebuilding the same in-memory trap in a new place. It is written for a NestJS app that already has a working gateway on one instance and now needs a second one.
A single Socket.io server holds every room, every socket, and every membership in its own process memory, so a broadcast to a room is a local loop over local sockets. Add a second instance and that loop still only covers local sockets, which means a message sent from instance A never reaches a room member connected to instance B. Nothing errors, no exception is thrown, and the sender's own test client often passes because it happens to be connected to the sending instance.
That silent half-delivery is the signature of this bug. If your chat, live feed, or collaboration room works for some users and not others, and the split lines up with which instance they landed on, you are almost certainly looking at in-memory rooms behind a load balancer rather than a bug in your room logic.
The Redis adapter replaces the in-memory room store with a shared channel that every instance listens to, so a broadcast becomes a publish step plus a local delivery step on each instance. When instance A emits to a room, it publishes that event to Redis; instance B receives it through its subscription and delivers it to its own local sockets in that room, and instance A does the same for its locals. Your gateway code does not change shape: server.to(room).emit(...) keeps working, and the adapter changes where that call reaches.
Two details in that picture matter for everything that follows. First, the adapter moves messages between servers, but it does not move your application state between servers. A map you built in instance A's memory is still invisible to instance B. Second, Redis becomes part of your real-time path, so a Redis outage is now a real-time outage unless you have planned for it. I cover both failure modes below, because the adapter solves the broadcast problem and creates two smaller problems you need to handle on purpose.
Start from the documented NestJS shape and do not improvise the wiring. Install the three packages, extend IoAdapter, connect two Redis clients, and register the adapter before the app starts listening. The two-client split is not optional decoration: a Redis connection in subscriber mode cannot issue regular commands, so the publishing client and the subscribing client must be separate connections, and duplicate() gives the subscriber the same configuration on its own connection.
npm install redis socket.io @socket.io/redis-adapter

// redis-io.adapter.ts
import { IoAdapter } from '@nestjs/platform-socket.io';
import { ServerOptions } from 'socket.io';
import { createAdapter } from '@socket.io/redis-adapter';
import { createClient } from 'redis';

export class RedisIoAdapter extends IoAdapter {
  private adapterConstructor: ReturnType<typeof createAdapter>;

  async connectToRedis(): Promise<void> {
    const pubClient = createClient({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL });
    const subClient = pubClient.duplicate();

    await Promise.all([pubClient.connect(), subClient.connect()]);
    this.adapterConstructor = createAdapter(pubClient, subClient);
  }

  createIOServer(port: number, options?: ServerOptions): any {
    const server = super.createIOServer(port, options);
    server.adapter(this.adapterConstructor);
    return server;
  }
}

// main.ts
import { NestFactory } from '@nestjs/core';
import { AppModule } from './app.module';
import { RedisIoAdapter } from './redis-io.adapter';

async function bootstrap() {
  const app = await NestFactory.create(AppModule);
  const redisIoAdapter = new RedisIoAdapter(app);
  await redisIoAdapter.connectToRedis();
  app.useWebSocketAdapter(redisIoAdapter);
  await app.listen(3000);
}
bootstrap();

Three things in that code deserve a second look before you ship it. Connect both clients before the app listens, or early broadcasts can run before the adapter is ready. Read the Redis URL from the environment, never hardcode localhost, or your staging and production setups will diverge in exactly the way that wastes a debugging afternoon. And keep the adapter construction in one place: every gateway in the app shares this adapter through useWebSocketAdapter, so a second copy of the wiring in another module is a second chance to drift. This is the same production NestJS stack I describe on my hiring page, where Socket.io and BullMQ on Redis are the real-time and background pair I ship with.
The Redis adapter moves broadcasts between instances, but it does not fix the handshake, and this is the step that sinks most first attempts at scaling Socket.io. A Socket.io client starts with HTTP long polling and then upgrades to a WebSocket connection, and those polling requests must reach the same instance that owns the session. If your load balancer round-robins a polling request to a different instance, that instance does not know the session, and the client gets errors and reconnect loops instead of a working socket.
The NestJS docs state the rule plainly: with multiple load-balanced instances you must either disable polling by setting transports: ['websocket'] in the client configuration, or enable cookie-based routing (sticky sessions) in your load balancer, because Redis alone is not enough. Pick one deliberately:



Approach
What it costs you
When I pick it




WebSocket-only transport
You lose the polling fallback, so clients behind strict proxies that block WebSocket upgrades cannot connect at all
Internal tools, controlled clients, or a first scaled deployment where you can measure how many clients actually need polling


Cookie-based sticky sessions
Your load balancer must support it and you must test it, since a misconfigured cookie rule fails exactly like no rule at all
Any public-facing app, because it keeps the polling fallback working while still pinning a session to one instance



Do not substitute IP-based hashing and call it done without thinking. Users behind corporate NAT, mobile networks, and shared Wi-Fi can share an IP or change it mid-session, and IP hashing pins them by an address that is not really theirs. A cookie set by the load balancer tracks the session, not the network path, and that is the property you actually need. Whichever option you pick, test it with polling enabled and with two instances running, because a sticky rule that only works in a single-instance test is not a sticky rule.
Once broadcasts cross instances, the next trap is addressing. The question stops being "how do I emit to a room" and becomes "how do I reach this one user, or this one tenant's room, when I have no idea which instance holds their socket". The answer is to stop asking which instance holds them. Address the room, and let the adapter find the instance.
The pattern I use has two rules. First, every socket joins a room named for its user immediately after authentication, before it joins anything else. Second, every shared room is named for the thing it belongs to, not for a server or a process, so a tenant room looks like tenant:9:project:41 and a user room looks like user:42. A service that needs to notify one user then emits to user:42 from any instance, and the adapter delivers it to whichever instance holds that socket.
// rooms.gateway.ts
import {
  WebSocketGateway,
  WebSocketServer,
  SubscribeMessage,
  OnGatewayConnection,
  MessageBody,
  ConnectedSocket,
} from '@nestjs/websockets';
import { Server, Socket } from 'socket.io';

@WebSocketGateway({ cors: { origin: process.env.CLIENT_ORIGIN } })
export class RoomsGateway implements OnGatewayConnection {
  @WebSocketServer()
  server: Server;

  async handleConnection(client: Socket) {
    // Authenticate first. Only join rooms for a verified user id.
    const userId = client.data.userId as string;
    await client.join(`user:${userId}`);
  }

  @SubscribeMessage('join-project')
  async onJoinProject(
    @MessageBody() projectId: string,
    @ConnectedSocket() client: Socket,
  ) {
    // Check membership in your database here, then join.
    await client.join(`project:${projectId}`);
    return { joined: projectId };
  }

  async notifyProject(projectId: string, payload: unknown) {
    // Callable from any instance. The adapter routes it.
    this.server.to(`project:${projectId}`).emit('project-updated', payload);
  }
}

That gateway sketch leaves two things deliberately out, and both are worth naming. Authentication is shown as a placeholder because token validation belongs in your own guard or handshake check, not in a copy-pasted snippet, and a socket that joins a room before authentication is a socket that can listen to a room it should never see. Membership checks are also a placeholder: joining project:41 must be gated on a database check that this user belongs to project 41, because a room name is not a permission. Validate the join payload before trusting it too; I use Zod at every boundary, as I wrote up in Zod as a contract. The adapter will faithfully deliver messages to anyone in the room, so the join step is where your access control lives.
One more habit pays off here. When you need to know who is actually connected, use the adapter's own tools, such as fetchSockets, instead of a local map. A local map answers "who is connected to this instance". The adapter answers "who is connected to this system", and at two or more instances only the second question is useful.
Socket.io now ships two Redis-backed adapters, and the choice between them is a real decision rather than a version detail. The classic adapter forwards packets through Redis pub/sub. The newer Redis Streams adapter forwards them through a Redis stream, and its docs give it one clear advantage: it handles a temporary disconnection from Redis and resumes the stream without losing packets, where the classic pub/sub adapter does not offer that resume behavior. Both still need sticky sessions, and the Streams docs say so directly, so the new adapter does not remove the handshake work from the previous section.



Adapter
How messages move
Disconnect behavior
NestJS wiring




Classic @socket.io/redis-adapter

Redis pub/sub, two clients
A gap during a Redis outage can lose packets
Documented in the NestJS adapter guide, shown above


@socket.io/redis-streams-adapter
One Redis stream, one client
Resumes the stream after a temporary disconnect without losing packets
Custom wiring through the same IoAdapter extension point, not the documented NestJS example



My pick is boring on purpose: start with the classic adapter from the NestJS docs. It is the documented path, it matches the examples other NestJS developers will recognize when they read your code, and its failure mode is one you can plan around with reconnect handling and client-side resync. Move to the Streams adapter when temporary Redis disconnects are a real problem in your deployment, not a theoretical one, and when you have time to verify the custom wiring against your own gateway tests. A newer adapter is not automatically the right adapter for a system that is already working.
A scaling change that has never been observed across two instances is a guess, and this one is cheap to observe. Run Redis locally, start your NestJS app twice on two ports, connect one client to each instance, and then trigger a broadcast from a service running on only one of them. If both clients receive it, the adapter is working. If only the client on the sending instance receives it, check the adapter registration first, then the Redis connection, then the room name, in that order, because those three cover most wiring mistakes.
Make the test adversarial in one specific way: connect with polling enabled, not websocket-only, and watch what happens when the load balancer is in the path. A setup that passes with a direct connection to one instance and fails behind the balancer has a sticky-session problem, not an adapter problem, and no amount of adapter debugging will fix it. The same test with websocket-only transport passing and polling failing points at the same place. Name the failure before you fix it, or you will fix the wrong layer twice.
Also verify the negative case. Disconnect a client, then broadcast to its room, and confirm nothing crashes and no stale delivery appears when it reconnects. Room membership is tied to the socket, not the user, so a reconnected client must rejoin its rooms after authentication. If your client code assumes rooms survive a reconnect, your users will silently stop receiving messages after every network drop, and that failure looks exactly like the original scaling bug.
Putting Redis in the middle of your real-time path changes what an outage means, so plan for it before it happens. The classic adapter has no stream to resume from, so messages published while Redis is unreachable do not wait politely for it to come back. Your clients need a resync path for exactly this case: on reconnect, the client asks for current state over HTTP or replays its room joins, and the server answers with the latest state rather than assuming the client saw every event. That resync endpoint is not extra polish. It is the thing that turns a Redis blip into a brief catch-up instead of a permanently stale screen.
Three more failure modes deserve a line each. First, keep payloads small. Every broadcast is published to Redis and then fanned out to every instance, so a large payload is multiplied by your instance count before it reaches a single client. Send ids and small deltas, and let clients fetch full objects over HTTP when they need them. Second, treat Redis as trusted internal infrastructure. The Socket.io docs warn that adapter messages are not signed or encrypted by the adapter, so do not expose Redis to untrusted networks, use authentication and TLS where your setup supports them, and never put long-lived secrets in event payloads. Third, watch the fan-out itself: a room with thousands of members across many instances turns one emit into thousands of deliveries, and that is a product decision as much as an infrastructure one. If a room grows that large, split it by topic or shard it by tenant before you scale the instance count to compensate.



Tool
What it is for
Note




Redis
The shared channel between Socket.io instances
Run it as managed infrastructure in production, not as a container you forget about on a single host


@socket.io/redis-adapter
The documented NestJS scaling path
The two-client setup above; keep its version aligned with your Socket.io version


@socket.io/redis-streams-adapter
The disconnect-resilient alternative
Evaluate it when Redis gaps become a measured problem, not before


A load balancer with cookie stickiness
Pinning polling sessions to one instance
Test it with polling enabled; IP hashing is a weaker substitute


Docker Compose
Running Redis plus two app instances locally
The cheapest way to observe the two-instance behavior before deploying it



{/* MONETIZATION NOTE: the tools table above is affiliate-ready. Swap the Redis and load-balancer rows for affiliate links only when a real program is joined; no affiliate claim is made here. */}
If your NestJS app has one instance today and needs two tomorrow, I would pick the classic Redis adapter from the NestJS docs, cookie-based sticky sessions at the load balancer, and room-per-user addressing from day one. That combination is documented, it is easy for the next developer to reason about, and it fixes the actual failure: broadcasts that only reach local sockets.
I would not pick the Streams adapter first, even though its disconnect behavior is better, because the documented path matters more than the newer feature when you are changing a live real-time system. I would not pick websocket-only transport for a public app, because giving up the polling fallback trades one visible failure for a quieter one on restrictive networks. And I would not ship any of it without the two-instance test above, because a scaling setup you have not watched fail and recover is a setup you do not understand yet.
The answer flips in two cases. If you already measure packet loss during Redis disconnects and users notice stale screens after every blip, the Streams adapter earns its custom wiring. If your clients are all internal and controlled, websocket-only transport is simpler than sticky sessions and the tradeoff disappears. Outside those cases, the boring documented combination is the right default, and boring is a compliment when the system in question is the one your users stare at all day.
If you are building a multi-tenant real-time system and want a second pair of eyes on the room design before it ships, that is the kind of production NestJS work I do. Reach me through the contact section on the homepage with the room model, the instance count, and the failure you are seeing, and we can walk through it in a first call.
By default each Socket.io server keeps its rooms in its own memory. A broadcast from instance A only reaches sockets connected to A, so a user on instance B never sees it. The Redis adapter fixes this by forwarding broadcasts through Redis, so every instance delivers the message to its own local sockets in that room.
Yes. The NestJS docs warn about this directly: Redis alone is not enough. Socket.io starts with HTTP long polling and then upgrades to WebSocket, and the polling requests must reach the same instance that owns the session. Either force websocket-only transport or turn on cookie-based routing at your load balancer.
A Redis connection in subscriber mode cannot issue regular commands, so one connection cannot both publish and subscribe. The standard setup creates a publishing client and then duplicates it for subscribing. That split is in the NestJS docs example and it is the shape to copy, not a detail to simplify away.
Start with the classic Redis adapter if you are following the NestJS docs, because that is the documented NestJS path. Consider the Redis Streams adapter when temporary Redis disconnects matter to you, because it resumes the stream without losing packets where the classic pub/sub adapter does not. Either way you still need sticky sessions.
Put every socket in a room named for its user, such as user:42, right after authentication. Then any instance can emit to that room and the adapter routes it to the instance holding that socket. Never keep a socketId to userId map in one instance's memory and expect other instances to read it.
Usually one of three things: the load balancer sends polling requests to the wrong instance, one instance holds state the others cannot see, or a broadcast payload grows until Redis fan-out becomes the bottleneck. Fix them in that order: sticky sessions first, room-based addressing second, and smaller payloads plus fewer broadcast rooms third.

Executive Summary


Chat assistants that only draft text are giving way to agents that act: they browse sites, fill in forms, run code and complete multi-step tasks. Three of the best-known examples are OpenAI’s computer-using agent (launched as Operator and now part of ChatGPT agent), Cognition’s Devin, and Manus. Teams are trying them on revenue-adjacent work such as researching accounts, benchmarking vendors, filling procurement and billing forms, and checking contract terms.
Enterprise sales workflows are rarely linear. An agent will hit an unlisted pricing tier, a custom security questionnaire or a contract value above its pre-approved limit. At that point, unchecked autonomy becomes a liability.
This guide describes a human-in-the-loop (HITL) control plane for those moments. A policy layer pauses the agent before a sensitive action, captures context, and posts an interactive Slack message to a sales “war room” channel. An account executive (AE) or revenue engineer can claim the deal, approve it, modify it or reject it. The agent then resumes with that decision, or the human takes over the session.
A caveat up front: you can only intercept an agent at the points where you control its execution. The article covers where those points are for each of these products, and which parts of the pattern are vendor features and which you build yourself.
Operator, Devin and Manus have all changed since they launched. Check the current state of each before designing around it.
OpenAI Operator → ChatGPT agent. OpenAI released Operator on January 23, 2025, as a research preview for ChatGPT Pro users in the US. It was powered by a model called Computer-Using Agent (CUA), which combines GPT-4o’s vision with reinforcement-learning-based reasoning to interact with graphical interfaces. On July 17, 2025, OpenAI folded Operator into ChatGPT as ChatGPT agent, which also absorbs deep research. OpenAI said the standalone operator.chatgpt.com site would be deprecated, and OpenAI’s Help Center now says the Operator website is no longer accessible. ChatGPT agent launched for Pro, Plus and Team users, and Enterprise and Edu plans followed on August 8, 2025.
Devin (Cognition). Devin is marketed as an autonomous AI software engineer that works with a shell, code editor and browser. Cognition acquired Windsurf in July 2025, and the Windsurf editor was rebranded Devin Desktop on June 2, 2026. Pricing has also changed. Public trackers list Free, Pro ($20/month), Max ($200/month), Teams ($80/month plus $40 per full developer seat) and custom Enterprise tiers as of mid-2026. Verify current pricing at devin.ai/pricing.
Manus. Manus launched in March 2025 as a general-purpose agent platform that browses the web, writes reports and executes tasks on a user’s behalf. Meta announced in December 2025 that it would acquire it for a reported $2 billion or more. China’s National Development and Reform Commission ordered the deal unwound on April 27, 2026. In August 2026 Manus said it would resume operating as an independent company. In October 2026 its parent, Butterfly Effect, announced a funding round of more than $500 million. If you build on Manus, plan for account and data changes during the separation: Manus warned in August that data generated by some users on or after December 29, 2025 would be deleted for regulatory reasons.
Computer-using agents run a loop of perception, reasoning and action. OpenAI’s CUA, for example, receives screenshots of the screen, reasons about the next step, and then issues mouse and keyboard actions. This is why such agents can work on websites that have no API. It also means they interact with pixels and UI elements rather than parsing the page’s DOM directly, which affects how you intercept them (see Section 2).
Their suitability for sales work differs:
ChatGPT agent: Works in a virtual browser and can fill in forms, research accounts and produce files. OpenAI states it is designed to ask for the user’s input at critical points, and Operator hands control back to the user for tasks such as logins and CAPTCHAs. OpenAI also said Operator was limited from higher-risk tasks such as banking transactions.
Devin: Best suited to technical work, such as auditing repositories, estimating migration effort, or running a proof of concept for a technical buyer. It is not a purchasing or contract-negotiation agent, so treat claims that it “evaluates licensing tiers” with caution.
Manus: A general-purpose research and execution agent, useful for market research, competitive benchmarking and report generation.
Agents handle standard workflows well, but enterprise transactions hit friction points that need a human:
Pricing or budget threshold exceeded. A renewal or expansion crosses a pre-authorized limit and needs sign-off.
Vendor gatekeeping. A vendor site presents a custom-quote form, a login challenge or a CAPTCHA mid-flow.
Custom legal or security terms. A procurement form asks for non-standard Master Services Agreement (MSA) language or a SOC 2 Type II report.
Non-standard discounting or bundling. A request falls outside approved discount tiers.
Routing these moments to people through Slack lets the agent do the repetitive work while humans keep authority over the high-stakes decisions.
How you intercept depends on who runs the agent:
Agents you orchestrate yourself. If you build on OpenAI’s computer-use tooling in its API, or on another agent framework, your code executes every action the model proposes. You can place a policy check between “model proposes action” and “action executes.” This is the most reliable place for a hard gate. OpenAI’s API tooling also supports safety checks that require the developer to acknowledge them before continuing.
Hosted products (ChatGPT agent, Devin, Manus). These run in the vendor’s own sandboxed environment, so you cannot insert a proxy into their browser. You can still use their built-in pause or takeover prompts and approval settings, restrict what the agent can reach through connectors and credentials, and scope its permissions so it cannot reach a purchase or signature step on its own. Where a product exposes an API or webhooks, use those events to trigger the Slack flow.
The pattern in this guide is the first case: a middleware state broker that sits in your own agent loop. For hosted agents, apply the same policy logic to whatever hooks and permissions the vendor exposes.
   +-----------------------+
   |   Agent Runtime       |
   | (your orchestrator or |
   |  vendor hook/webhook) |
   +-----------+-----------+
               |  proposed action
               v
  +--------------------------+
  |  Middleware Broker       |  <--- Evaluates rules:
  |  (State & Policy Engine) |       - Deal value > threshold?
  +------------+-------------+       - Unknown legal / security field?
               |                     - Login, CAPTCHA or payment step?
       +-------+-------+
       | Intercept     |
       v               v
  +----------+   +-------------------+
  | Pause    |   | Post Slack        |
  | Session  |   | Interactive Card  |
  +----------+   +---------+---------+
                           |
                           v
                 +-------------------+
                 | Sales War Room    |
                 |  (Slack channel)  |
                 +---------+---------+
                           |
                           | AE clicks "Claim"
                           v
                 +-------------------+
                 | Resume / Handoff  |
                 +-------------------+

State interceptor. Wraps the agent’s action loop. It records the proposed action, the current step, the intent log and a screenshot before anything executes.
Policy and risk engine. Evaluates each proposed action against business rules such as maximum deal value, maximum discount, restricted regions, and a list of actions that always require approval (sending payment details, accepting terms, signing).
Slack messaging gateway. Turns the intercepted context into a Block Kit message with action buttons.
Session store and resume handlers. Persist the agent’s state (task, plan, memory buffer, browser session reference) while the agent waits, with a timeout so stale sessions do not linger. Never store credentials or payment data in the Slack payload.
The following Node.js/TypeScript example shows the pattern. It is a blueprint rather than production code: the session-store, pause and resume functions are placeholders for your own infrastructure. It uses Slack’s Bolt for JavaScript framework, which verifies Slack’s request signatures for you.
// agentInterceptor.ts
import { sendSlackWarRoomAlert } from './slackAlert';

export interface AgentState {
  agentId: string;
  agentRuntime: string;        // e.g. "custom-cua", "devin", "manus"
  targetCompany: string;
  proposedACV: number;         // annual contract value in USD
  currentStep: string;
  sessionId: string;
  screenshotUrl: string;       // must be a publicly reachable HTTPS URL (see 3.2)
}

// Illustrative policy: tune to your own approval matrix.
const APPROVAL_THRESHOLD_USD = 25_000;

export async function evaluateAgentAction(state: AgentState): Promise<'proceed' | 'halted'> {
  if (state.proposedACV >= APPROVAL_THRESHOLD_USD) {
    await pauseAgentSession(state.sessionId);
    await sendSlackWarRoomAlert(state);
    return 'halted';
  }
  return 'proceed';
}

async function pauseAgentSession(sessionId: string): Promise<void> {
  // Persist state and stop issuing actions for this session.
  // Add an expiry (for example 30 minutes) after which the session is
  // aborted or escalated, so paused sessions never hang indefinitely.
}

Slack’s image block requires a publicly accessible image_url, so upload screenshots to storage that Slack can reach (for example a short-lived signed URL). Screenshots may contain customer data, so apply your retention and access rules.
// slackAlert.ts
import { WebClient } from '@slack/web-api';
import type { AgentState } from './agentInterceptor';

const slack = new WebClient(process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN);
const WAR_ROOM_CHANNEL = process.env.SLACK_WAR_ROOM_CHANNEL_ID!;

export async function sendSlackWarRoomAlert(state: AgentState) {
  const deal = `$${state.proposedACV.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;

  await slack.chat.postMessage({
    channel: WAR_ROOM_CHANNEL,
    text: `High-value deal paused for review: ${state.targetCompany} (${deal})`, // notification fallback
    blocks: [
      {
        type: 'header',
        text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Agent paused: human review required', emoji: true },
      },
      {
        type: 'section',
        fields: [
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Account:*\n${state.targetCompany}` },
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Detected deal size:*\n${deal}` },
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Agent:*\n${state.agentRuntime}` },
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Current step:*\n${state.currentStep}` },
        ],
      },
      {
        type: 'image',
        title: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Browser state at pause' },
        image_url: state.screenshotUrl,
        alt_text: 'Screenshot of the agent browser at the moment it was paused',
      },
      {
        type: 'actions',
        block_id: `agent_actions_${state.sessionId}`,
        elements: [
          {
            type: 'button',
            text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Claim & take over', emoji: true },
            style: 'primary',
            value: state.sessionId,
            action_id: 'claim_deal_session',
          },
          {
            type: 'button',
            text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Approve & resume', emoji: true },
            value: state.sessionId,
            action_id: 'approve_agent_action',
          },
          {
            type: 'button',
            text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Reject & abort', emoji: true },
            style: 'danger',
            value: state.sessionId,
            action_id: 'reject_agent_action',
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
  });
}

Slack expects an acknowledgment of an interaction within three seconds, so Bolt’s ack() is called first and the slower work follows. Claiming must be atomic, otherwise two AEs can click at once and both believe they own the deal.
// slackHandlers.ts
import { App } from '@slack/bolt';

const app = new App({
  token: process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN,
  signingSecret: process.env.SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET, // Bolt verifies request signatures
});

app.action('claim_deal_session', async ({ ack, body, client }) => {
  await ack();
  const action = (body as any).actions[0];
  const sessionId: string = action.value;
  const userId = body.user.id;

  // Atomic claim: only the first caller succeeds (e.g. a conditional write in your database).
  const claimed = await tryClaimSession(sessionId, userId);
  if (!claimed) {
    await client.chat.postEphemeral({
      channel: (body as any).channel.id,
      user: userId,
      text: 'Someone else has already claimed this session.',
    });
    return;
  }

  await client.chat.update({
    channel: (body as any).channel.id,
    ts: (body as any).message.ts,
    text: `Session ${sessionId} claimed by <@${userId}>`,
    blocks: [
      {
        type: 'section',
        text: { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Session claimed by <@${userId}>.* Human control is active.` },
      },
      {
        type: 'context',
        elements: [{ type: 'mrkdwn', text: `Session ID: \`${sessionId}\`` }],
      },
    ],
  });

  // Give the AE access through your own authenticated, expiring session viewer.
  const url = await createHandoffUrl(sessionId, userId);
  await client.chat.postEphemeral({
    channel: (body as any).channel.id,
    user: userId,
    text: `You now control session \`${sessionId}\`: ${url}`,
  });
});

app.action('approve_agent_action', async ({ ack, body }) => {
  await ack();
  const sessionId: string = (body as any).actions[0].value;
  await recordDecision(sessionId, body.user.id, 'approved'); // audit log
  await resumeAgentSession(sessionId);
});

app.action('reject_agent_action', async ({ ack, body }) => {
  await ack();
  const sessionId: string = (body as any).actions[0].value;
  await recordDecision(sessionId, body.user.id, 'rejected');
  await abortAgentSession(sessionId);
});

// --- Placeholders for your own infrastructure ---
async function tryClaimSession(sessionId: string, userId: string): Promise<boolean> { return true; }
async function createHandoffUrl(sessionId: string, userId: string): Promise<string> {
  // Return a short-lived, authenticated link to your remote-browser viewer
  // (for example one built on a WebRTC or VNC-based stack). Do not embed
  // the user ID as the credential.
  return `https://warroom.example.com/session/${sessionId}`;
}
async function recordDecision(sessionId: string, userId: string, decision: string) {}
async function resumeAgentSession(sessionId: string) {}
async function abortAgentSession(sessionId: string) {}

(async () => { await app.start(process.env.PORT || 3000); })();

Design notes:
Restrict who can approve. Check the clicking user against your approval matrix (role, region, deal-size authority) before resuming. Slack channel membership alone is not authorization.
Treat the handoff link as a credential. It should be authenticated, short-lived and single-use.
Keep an audit trail. Log every pause, claim, approval and rejection with a timestamp and user ID, and write a summary to your CRM (for example Salesforce or HubSpot) through its API.
The thresholds below are illustrative. Set them from your own approval matrix.



Trigger
Agent action
Interception condition
War room protocol




Large deal value
Fills billing data, selects a standard tier
Proposed ACV at or above your threshold (for example $25,000)

Claim: The account’s AE, or a round-robin AE, claims the card and decides whether to approve, change terms or take over.


Non-standard legal terms
Detects an unrecognized clause or terms field
Any unrecognized legal checkbox or free-text contract field

Legal routing: Card goes to a shared legal and revenue-operations channel.


Seat or tier limit exceeded
Reaches the maximum seat allocation
Seats requested above your policy limit

Review: AE reviews and may offer a custom package.


Login, CAPTCHA or bot challenge
Encounters a verification step
Agent signals it cannot proceed, or a challenge page is detected

Human handoff: A person completes the step through a remote view and hands back. Do not engineer ways around such challenges.


Payment or signature step
Reaches a payment, contract acceptance or e-signature screen
Always

Mandatory approval: Never let an agent complete these without human sign-off.



Potential benefits
Faster handling of routine work. Agents can take on data gathering, form completion and research, so people focus on negotiation and approvals. The share of work an agent can absorb varies widely by workflow, so measure it in your own pilot rather than assuming a fixed figure.
Faster escalation. A Slack notification reaches the right person in seconds, but how quickly the deal moves still depends on whether someone responds. Set response-time targets and an escalation path, such as re-pinging after a few minutes and falling back to a backup AE.
Auditability and governance. Logged pauses, claims and overrides, with screenshots, give compliance and sales leadership a record of who approved what.
Less manual entry for AEs. Reps spend less time on procurement paperwork and more on relationship work.
Limits and risks
Agents still make mistakes. OpenAI describes its agent products as capable but fallible, and says it designs them to ask for confirmation before consequential actions. That is why the approval gate belongs in your control plane and not only in the vendor’s defaults.
Prompt injection from web pages is a real risk for browsing agents. Do not give an agent credentials or tool access beyond what the task needs, and keep payment and signature actions behind human approval.
Vendor products change quickly. Operator was retired as a standalone product, Windsurf was rebranded, and Manus changed ownership twice within a year. Keep the policy and Slack layers independent of any one agent so you can swap the runtime.
Review data-handling terms for customer data, screenshots and transcripts before sending them to Slack or a third-party agent.
Autonomous agents such as ChatGPT agent (the successor to Operator), Devin and Manus can shorten the repetitive parts of the revenue cycle, but enterprise deals need human judgment at the points where money, legal terms or customer relationships are on the line. A control plane that pauses the agent, packages the context and routes it to a Slack war room lets a person step in at those moments and then hand work back to the agent.
Decide where you can intercept: your own agent loop, vendor webhooks, or permission scoping for hosted agents.
Define an approval matrix covering deal size, discount limits, legal terms, regions and who may approve each.
Build the policy engine, session store and audit log first, then the Slack layer.
Create the Slack app with Block Kit messages and interactivity, with signature verification and atomic claiming.
Pilot on a low-risk workflow, track pause rate, time to claim and override rate, and tighten the policies from the data.
Train AEs on claiming sessions and taking over via the handoff viewer.
