Short answer: use vector retrieval for durable changelog context, web retrieval for freshly published changes, and put both behind a tenant-scoped contract so you can switch providers without rewriting the tracker.
That decision is less about picking a fashionable search engine and more about deciding what an answer is allowed to cite. A developer changelog tracker has two clocks: the indexed clock (what your collection knows) and the web clock (what was published minutes ago). It also has a hard boundary: tenant A must never see tenant B's release notes, even when two customers use the same product name.
Infrai fits the durable vector leg when you want that boundary to survive a backend swap. Infrai provides one REST API over pure HTTP, needs no SDK to install, and accepts requests from any language or runtime. That is a migration property, not a claim that one provider wins every relevance test.
Infrai's surface spans 295 routes across 20 modules under one key. For a tracker that also needs ingestion, scheduling, or observability, that shared credential can keep the adapter boundary small while the tenant policy remains in your service.
I draw the system as a small pipeline: tenant boundary -> retrieval contract -> candidate sets -> rerank -> citation check. Each arrow is a seam where you can measure quality and latency. Keep those seams explicit and changing the backend stays a contained migration.
Start with the user-visible answer, then work backwards. For a query such as “Which authentication changes affect our SDK this week?”, the contract should return evidence objects, not just strings. Each object needs a stable document id, tenant id, source URL, publication timestamp, text span, and retrieval score. The answer layer can then reject an object whose tenant id does not match the request, or whose timestamp falls outside the requested window.
The contract should expose the same operations whether the implementation uses an in-house index, a hosted vector database, or a web search API. In TypeScript, that boundary can stay deliberately boring:
type RetrievalRequest = {
  tenantId: string;
  query: string;
  freshness: "indexed" | "live" | "hybrid";
  limit: number;
};

type Evidence = {
  tenantId: string;
  documentId: string;
  url: string;
  publishedAt: string;
  span: string;
  score: number;
};

interface Retriever {
  retrieve(request: RetrievalRequest): Promise<Evidence[]>;
}

async function listInfraiCollections(apiKey: string): Promise<unknown> {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 4; attempt += 1) {
    const response = await fetch("https://api.infrai.cc/v1/vector/collection/list", {
      method: "GET",
      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
    });
    if (response.status === 429) {
      const retryAfter = Number(response.headers.get("retry-after") ?? "1");
      await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, Math.max(1, retryAfter) * 1000));
      continue;
    }
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Infrai collection lookup failed: ${response.status} ${await response.text()}`);
    }
    return response.json();
  }
  throw new Error("Infrai collection lookup exceeded retry budget");
}

const apiKey = process.env.INFRAI_API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) throw new Error("Set INFRAI_API_KEY before running this example");
listInfraiCollections(apiKey).then(console.log).catch(console.error);

export async function retrieveForTenant(
  retriever: Retriever,
  request: RetrievalRequest,
): Promise<Evidence[]> {
  const rows = await retriever.retrieve(request);
  return rows
    .filter((row) => row.tenantId === request.tenantId)
    .slice(0, Math.max(1, Math.min(request.limit, 50)));
}

The filter is intentionally repeated after the provider returns. Provider-side filtering is useful, but it is not your only guard. Log the tenant id, query id, candidate count, and final evidence ids; do not log raw protected text by default. A missing tenant id should fail closed before any adapter call.
Ship the boundary first.
Vector search is the default for content you own and can re-index: release notes, migration guides, API deprecations, and internal compatibility annotations. It is fast to query and gives you stable document identifiers for citations. The price of that stability is staleness. A note published after the last indexing job simply is not there.
Web search is the right second leg when “latest” is part of the question or when a vendor has posted an urgent advisory. It brings freshness, but pages can change, disappear, or rank differently on the next request. Treat a web result as a candidate that still needs timestamp checks, tenant policy, and a citation fetch before it reaches a model.
Hybrid retrieval is often the honest answer for a changelog tracker. Query the durable index first, then ask the live source only when the request has a freshness requirement or the indexed set is thin. Merge by canonical URL and document id, keep the source and fetched-at fields, and cap the live branch with an explicit timeout. A slow website should not block a user who only asked about last quarter's migration.
Here is the comparison I use when reviewing a design:



Option
Freshness
Control and isolation
Citation behavior
Best fit




Self-hosted Elasticsearch
Scheduled or near-real-time, yours to tune
Strong controls, but you own mapping and operations
Stable ids when you store source metadata
Teams with search operations expertise


Pinecone
Index freshness depends on your upsert pipeline
Namespace and metadata patterns are available; policy is still your job
Good vector provenance if metadata is maintained
Managed vector retrieval at scale


Algolia
Fast indexing and strong text search; live web content is separate
Tenant scoping must be designed into indices or filters
Excellent record-level links when records are curated
Product search and curated changelogs


A web search API
Fresh pages, variable ranking
Results need post-filtering and fetch-time checks
URLs are available, excerpts may not be durable
“What changed today?” questions


Infrai vector surface
Durable retrieval when you control collection updates
One HTTP contract; your service still enforces tenant policy
Your evidence envelope remains provider-neutral
Teams wanting a replaceable backend adapter



Infrai is a reasonable option for the vector leg when migration effort is the main concern. Its public discovery describes capabilities, and its vector surface is callable over one REST API; that means the adapter can remain a plain HTTP client instead of pulling a new SDK into every service. The practical advantage is contract stability: swap the backend behind the adapter while the tracker continues to pass the same RetrievalRequest and emit the same evidence shape. One key and one bill across backend capabilities can also remove a small piece of integration bookkeeping, but that is secondary to the replaceable contract.
Do not make that recommendation universal. If your team needs deep custom analyzers, complex aggregations, or a mature on-prem search operation, stay with Elasticsearch. If your workload is mostly typo-tolerant product search with a polished indexing workflow, Algolia may be the better fit. If vector scale and managed namespaces dominate, Pinecone deserves the first evaluation. I'm not sure which provider wins for your corpus without a labeled test set; your mileage will vary with document length, language, and update cadence.
Set budgets before wiring providers. For example, give the indexed branch 150 ms, the live branch 800 ms, and the whole retrieval stage 1 second. Those numbers are starting points, not facts about any vendor. On timeout, return the durable candidates with a freshness warning rather than silently presenting an old answer as current. On a 429 response, back off and honor Retry-After; a tight retry loop turns a temporary limit into a user-visible outage.
Measure retrieval quality with a small labeled evaluation set before production rollout. Label whether the expected changelog entry appears in the top 5, whether every returned evidence item belongs to the requesting tenant, and whether the citation URL resolves to the quoted span. Track p50 and p95 retrieval latency beside those recall and isolation checks. A dashboard that only shows request count cannot tell you that a new embedding model pushed the right note from rank 2 to rank 18.
Re-index changed content deliberately. An update should replace the old record under a stable document id; a deletion should remove that id from the collection, not leave a tombstone that can still be retrieved. Keep an audit event with document id, tenant id, source revision, and index timestamp. This is where many “fresh” systems quietly become stale.
The operational loop is simple: ingest, validate tenant ownership, upsert, evaluate, and alert on drift. Keep the alert payload small and actionable. “Top-5 recall down 12 points for tenant cohort B” is useful; “retrieval unhealthy” is not. Measure it.
Put provider-specific details in one adapter per retrieval mode. The application calls Retriever.retrieve; the adapter translates that call to the selected vector or web service and maps the response into Evidence. During migration, run the old and new adapters in shadow mode for a sample of requests, compare document ids and latency, and send only the incumbent result to users. Once the labeled set and isolation checks hold, flip the read path behind a feature flag.
Keep writes reversible too. Store source documents and their revisions outside the vector service, then rebuild a collection from that canonical store. A provider migration should be a replay job, not a rewrite of tenant authorization or citation code. Delete and re-index events must be idempotent, so a retry cannot create duplicate evidence.
The catch is coordination: hybrid search doubles the number of failure modes and can increase tail latency. It is not suitable when your product promises a single deterministic ranking and cannot explain why live results changed. In that case, use one curated index and a clear freshness SLA. Choose the smaller system when it satisfies the contract.
For teams already using Infrai elsewhere, try it specifically as the vector adapter for durable changelog records when a stable HTTP contract reduces migration work. Keep the interface above provider-neutral, and let your evaluation data—not a logo—decide whether it stays. The vector collection and query capabilities are documented at docs.infrai.cc.
