Omnichannel Chatbot Flow Design Around WhatsApp Messaging Rules (and Every Other Platform's)


You design one beautiful chatbot flow. It welcomes the user, qualifies them, sends a follow-up the next day, and closes with an offer. It works perfectly in your web widget.
Then you ship it to WhatsApp, and the follow-up silently fails. On Instagram, the offer gets blocked. On Telegram, you can't even start the conversation.
The flow wasn't broken. It ignored the platform's messaging rules.
In this guide you'll learn how to approach omnichannel chatbot flow design around WhatsApp messaging rules and the rules of other channels: how to model each platform's constraints, build a single policy layer that decides what you're allowed to send, and design flows that degrade gracefully instead of failing.
Why one flow per channel doesn't scale
Prerequisites
The rules that actually shape your flows
Step 1: Model each channel as a capability profile
Step 2: Build a send-policy check
Step 3: Design flows with fallbacks, not forks
Step 4: Handle the 24-hour window in automation
Common mistakes
Conclusion
The obvious approach to omnichannel automation is to copy your flow once per platform and tweak it. It works for two channels. By the fourth, you're maintaining four diverging versions of the same logic, and every business change (a new offer, a new qualifying question) has to be made four times.
The opposite approach, one flow with no awareness of channels, fails the moment a platform says no.
The sustainable middle path: one flow definition, one policy layer, many channel adapters. The flow describes intent ("remind the user about their cart"). The policy layer decides whether and how that intent can be delivered on the current channel.
Before you start, you should have:
[ ] Basic familiarity with webhooks and REST APIs
[ ] A chatbot or automation platform that handles more than one channel (or plans to)
[ ] Access to the official docs for each channel you support (rules change, so treat them as the source of truth)
[ ] Comfort reading TypeScript (the examples are small and easy to port)
Note: Platform rules, pricing and template categories change regularly. Everything below is a design pattern. Always verify specifics against each platform's current documentation before shipping.
You don't need to memorize every policy. You need to know which kinds of rules affect flow design:



Rule type
What it means for your flow
Example




Who speaks first
Can the bot start a conversation?
Telegram bots can't message a user who hasn't started the bot


Messaging window
How long can you reply freely after the user's last message?
WhatsApp and Instagram use a 24-hour window


Pre-approved templates
What must you use outside the window?
WhatsApp requires approved templates


Opt-in requirements
What proof of consent do you need?
WhatsApp requires opt-in before business-initiated messages


Content limits
Buttons, list sizes, media types
Each channel caps buttons and quick replies differently


Rate limits
How fast can you send?
Telegram throttles broadcasts per second



The WhatsApp messaging rules are usually the strictest, which is why we design around them first. If a flow survives WhatsApp, it usually survives everywhere else.
On WhatsApp, when a user messages you, a customer service window opens (24 hours from their last message). Inside it, you can send free-form replies. Outside it, you can only send pre-approved template messages, and those are categorized (for example marketing, utility, authentication), which affects approval and cost.
Design implication: every flow step that happens "later" (reminders, follow-ups, re-engagement) needs a template-based fallback.
Instagram DMs and Facebook Messenger also follow a messaging-window model, and Instagram conversations start from the user's action (a DM, a comment reply, or a story reply). There's no cold outreach.
Design implication: your flow's entry points must be user-triggered. Comment-to-DM and story-reply automations exist for this reason.
Telegram doesn't use a 24-hour window or templates. Instead, the user must start the bot first, and after that you can message them freely (within rate limits and Telegram's terms).
Design implication: the "start" event is your opt-in. Capture it, store it, and treat blocking the bot as an opt-out.
Stop scattering if (channel === "whatsapp") checks through your flows. Describe each channel once, as data.
type ChannelProfile = {
  id: "whatsapp" | "instagram" | "telegram" | "web";
  canInitiate: boolean;          // can the bot start a conversation?
  windowHours: number | null;    // free-form reply window, null = none
  requiresTemplateOutsideWindow: boolean;
  maxQuickReplies: number;
  maxMessagesPerSecond: number;
};

export const channels: Record<string, ChannelProfile> = {
  whatsapp: {
    id: "whatsapp",
    canInitiate: true,              // only with opt-in + approved template
    windowHours: 24,
    requiresTemplateOutsideWindow: true,
    maxQuickReplies: 3,
    maxMessagesPerSecond: 20,
  },
  instagram: {
    id: "instagram",
    canInitiate: false,
    windowHours: 24,
    requiresTemplateOutsideWindow: false, // check current docs for tags/exceptions
    maxQuickReplies: 13,
    maxMessagesPerSecond: 10,
  },
  telegram: {
    id: "telegram",
    canInitiate: false,             // user must /start first
    windowHours: null,
    requiresTemplateOutsideWindow: false,
    maxQuickReplies: 8,
    maxMessagesPerSecond: 30,
  },
  web: {
    id: "web",
    canInitiate: true,
    windowHours: null,
    requiresTemplateOutsideWindow: false,
    maxQuickReplies: 6,
    maxMessagesPerSecond: 50,
  },
};

Tip: The numbers above are illustrative. Load real values from configuration so you can update them without redeploying when platforms change their limits.
The policy layer answers one question before every outbound message: "Am I allowed to send this, here, now?"
type SendDecision =
  | { action: "send_freeform" }
  | { action: "send_template"; reason: string }
  | { action: "blocked"; reason: string };

export function decideSend(opts: {
  channel: ChannelProfile;
  lastUserMessageAt: Date | null;
  userHasOptedIn: boolean;
  now?: Date;
}): SendDecision {
  const { channel, lastUserMessageAt, userHasOptedIn } = opts;
  const now = opts.now ?? new Date();

  // Rule 1: never message someone who hasn't consented
  if (!userHasOptedIn) {
    return { action: "blocked", reason: "no_opt_in" };
  }

  // Rule 2: channels with no window are always free-form
  if (channel.windowHours === null) {
    return { action: "send_freeform" };
  }

  // Rule 3: no inbound message ever => bot can't initiate (unless allowed)
  if (!lastUserMessageAt) {
    return channel.canInitiate && channel.requiresTemplateOutsideWindow
      ? { action: "send_template", reason: "business_initiated" }
      : { action: "blocked", reason: "cannot_initiate" };
  }

  // Rule 4: inside the window => free-form
  const hoursSince =
    (now.getTime() - lastUserMessageAt.getTime()) / 36e5;
  if (hoursSince <= channel.windowHours) {
    return { action: "send_freeform" };
  }

  // Rule 5: outside the window
  return channel.requiresTemplateOutsideWindow
    ? { action: "send_template", reason: "window_expired" }
    : { action: "blocked", reason: "window_expired" };
}

Now every outbound message in every flow goes through decideSend. Your flows never need to know the rules; they only react to the decision.
A fork is a separate flow per channel. A fallback is one flow with a defined plan B for each step.
For each step in your flow, define:
Primary action: what you want to do (free-form message with buttons)
Template fallback: what to do if the window is closed (approved template with a re-engagement button)
Channel fallback: what to do if the channel can't deliver it at all (switch to another channel the user has opted into)
Silent fallback: what to do if nothing works (log it, stop, don't retry forever)




step: cart_reminder
intent: remind_user_about_cart
primary:
  type: freeform
  text: "You left something in your cart. Want to finish checkout?"
  quick_replies: ["Checkout", "Not now"]
fallbacks:
  window_expired:
    type: template
    template_name: cart_reminder_v2
  cannot_initiate:
    type: channel_switch
    prefer: ["telegram", "email"]
  blocked:
    type: log_and_stop

The same step now works on WhatsApp (template after 24 hours), Instagram (skip or switch), and Telegram (free-form anytime).
Content limits are the second source of breakage. Write your flow with the richest representation, then let each adapter downgrade it:
function adaptQuickReplies(options: string[], channel: ChannelProfile) {
  if (options.length <= channel.maxQuickReplies) return { type: "buttons", options };
  // Too many options: fall back to a numbered text menu
  return {
    type: "text_menu",
    text: options.map((o, i) => `${i + 1}. ${o}`).join("\n"),
  };
}

Six buttons become a numbered list on a channel that only supports three. The flow stays identical; only the presentation changes.
The window is where most WhatsApp automation quietly breaks. Three patterns help:
Don't save the important message for "tomorrow." If the user's engagement is the thing opening the window, use it. Deliver the key answer while the window is open.
When a flow says "wait 2 days, then follow up," compute whether the window will still be open at send time:
function planFollowUp(lastUserMessageAt: Date, delayHours: number, channel: ChannelProfile) {
  const sendAt = new Date(lastUserMessageAt.getTime() + delayHours * 36e5);
  const windowEnd = channel.windowHours
    ? new Date(lastUserMessageAt.getTime() + channel.windowHours * 36e5)
    : null;

  return {
    sendAt,
    willNeedTemplate: windowEnd !== null && sendAt > windowEnd,
  };
}

If willNeedTemplate is true, validate at design time that an approved template exists. Don't discover that in production.
A template message that asks a simple question ("Want to continue? Reply YES") is the cheapest way to reopen the conversation. When the user replies, the window reopens and your full free-form flow resumes.
Hardcoding channel checks inside flows. Use capability profiles and a policy layer instead.
Treating templates as an afterthought. Template approval takes time; plan them alongside the flow.
Ignoring opt-out signals. Blocking the bot, "STOP" replies and unsubscribe keywords must all feed one consent record.
Retrying blocked messages. Repeated failed sends can hurt your quality rating. Log and stop.
Designing for the richest channel only. Always test the downgraded version of every message.
Treating the rules as static. Platform policies evolve. Keep rules in config and review them regularly.
Great omnichannel chatbot flow design isn't about squeezing the same conversation into every channel. It's about separating what you want to say from what each platform lets you say.
Key takeaways:
Design one flow and put channel differences into capability profiles, not into the flow itself.
Use a send-policy layer so every outbound message is checked against opt-in, windows and templates.
Plan fallbacks (template, channel switch, log and stop) for every time-based step.
Treat WhatsApp messaging rules as your design baseline, since a flow that survives them will usually work across your other channels.
Keep platform rules in configuration, because they will change.
Whether you're building chatbots by hand or using an automation platform like Botsailor, the principle is the same: respect each channel's rules, and your omnichannel automation will feel reliable, compliant and consistent across WhatsApp, Instagram and Telegram.
What's the strictest platform rule you've had to design around? Share it in the comments. I'd love to compare notes.

The Problem with any in Legacy Codebases


During rapid migrations from JavaScript to TypeScript, developers frequently annotate complex data structures with any. While this allows the project to compile immediately, it completely disables TypeScript's static type checker, hiding potential runtime exceptions and breaking downstream automation tools like Auto-Doc AI.
Overusing any introduces technical debt that trickles down to modern pipelines. It destroys IDE IntelliSense support and prevents documentation engines from inferring true domain models.
In the following snippet, an API payload is handled using an implicit or explicit any type. There is no runtime checking, type safety, or structured TSDoc metadata.
// ❌ Legacy untyped code susceptible to runtime errors
function processUserData(data: any) {
  console.log("User ID:", data.id);
  if (data.role === "admin") {
    return data.permissions.includes("write");
  }
  return false;
}

To align with TypeScript's strict mode requirements and enable Auto-Doc AI to generate automated API specifications, we replace any with unknown and implement a custom Type Guard alongside formatted TSDoc comments.
/**
 * Validated user permissions structure.
 */
export interface UserPayload {
  id: string;
  role: 'admin' | 'user';
  permissions: string[];
}

/**
 * Custom type guard predicate verifying object structure.
 *
 * @param data - Unstructured payload from remote API.
 * @returns `true` if payload conforms to {@link UserPayload}.
 */
export function isUserPayload(data: unknown): data is UserPayload {
  return (
    typeof data === 'object' &&
    data !== null &&
    'id' in data &&
    typeof (data as Record<string, unknown>).id === 'string' &&
    'role' in data &&
    ['admin', 'user'].includes((data as Record<string, unknown>).role as string) &&
    'permissions' in data &&
    Array.isArray((data as Record<string, unknown>).permissions)
  );
}

/**
 * Evaluates user write privileges with strict runtime validation.
 *
 * @param data - Raw unvalidated input object.
 * @returns True if user has active write permission.
 * @throws {TypeError} If payload structure validation fails.
 */
export function processUserDataStrict(data: unknown): boolean {
  if (!isUserPayload(data)) {
    throw new TypeError('Invalid payload structure: does not satisfy UserPayload');
  }
  if (data.role === 'admin') {
    return data.permissions.includes('write');
  }
  return false;
}

Ensure code consistency across large scale enterprise applications by adding JSDoc/TSDoc formatting plugins to your .prettierrc configuration:
{
  "singleQuote": true,
  "trailingComma": "all",
  "printWidth": 100,
  "tsdoc": true
}

Replace any with unknown: Forces developers to narrow types safely prior to accessing properties.
Leverage Type Predicates (data is Type): Enables the TypeScript compiler to infer types inside validated conditional blocks.
Standardize with TSDoc: Explicit @param, @returns, and @throws tags empower Auto-Doc AI to generate complete API documentation automatically.
Enforce Rules via CI/CD: Run Prettier and strict TypeScript compilation checks on every pull request.
Try it interactively: Format, validate, and auto-document your typescript directly in JW Solutions Code Formatter & Auto-Doc AI.
